9.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: In the Russian original the thing that is always rendered as ‘coat’ / ‘overcoat’ in English is Ochumelov’s шинель. The word шинель / the overcoat is brought on stage with Ochumelov and then is used by Chekhov almost as a barometer of the inspector’s chameleon-like self-protection.

In the text as it is normally printed the шинель is named / is made visible about seven times and it is given a really descriptive adjective on four distinct occasions, which are the three/four psychological shifts that the story is built on. 

In the Garnett / Constance Garnett-type text the four descriptive moments are:

* the arrival in the new / smart overcoat when Ochumelov is still externally self-confident;
* the coat thrown open / buttoned up as he begins to get nervous and begins to feel the need to impress;
* the coat fussed with / taken off and put on again as opinion about the dog and its owner is reversed;
* the coat seized and flung on as he makes his final, self-protective exit.

That is the set of descriptive passages that literary commentators normally isolate; it is generally summed up as the three basic shifts of confidence → anxiety → panic → flight, i.e. four descriptive mentions of the overcoat, seven bare mentions of the garment in all. If the bare mentions are counted the number is ~5-7 depending on the edition/translation.

