8.text:
The coat is a leitmotif, not an inventory list. In the story as it is normally printed the garment is brought back only when Ochumelov’s “colour” changes, and the changes that criticism isolates are three narratively meaningful descriptive uses of the coat, the three classic chameleon shifts.

* Entrance / baseline self-importance: Ochumelov comes on in the new / smart frock-coat / overcoat / surtout, the coat marking his desire to be seen as respectable and in authority;
* Middle vacillation / nervous handling: the coat is fidgeted with, buttoned / unbuttoned, wiped on, etc., as the judgment of the dog and the crowd’s pressure makes him uncertain and he tries to protect himself;
* Exit / capitulation: the coat is again described as he wraps himself in it / leaves the square, the final protective, servile posture after the last reversal.

That is the three-stage symbolic register of fearful mentality that is stable across editions. In the Constance Garnett / canonical Russian text that three-fold descriptive work is rendered as four nominal punctuations of шинель / overcoat / surtout / greatcoat in all, the three descriptive moments above being the ones that are normally read as the symbolic, fear-driven shifts. Raw nominal count is edition/translation dependent – about 4–5 mentions in the usual English printing and ~5–7 bare mentions of шинель/пальто in Russian – but the literary point is the three distinct descriptive shifts.

